Correction — one authored model, hardware LOD, programmable Zixxtrixx choreography

You are right. My previous LOD section was architecturally wrong.

I described a separately targeted “LOD0 model” and “micro LOD model” as though the creature agent should author and optimize both. Delete that entire idea.

1. The actual LOD architecture

Zixxtrixx needs one production model.

The repository divides the work correctly:

The modeller authors one source creature.
compile_creature expands that model into the full mesh, automatically derives the micro representation by retaining alternating rings and halving circumference segments, and measures the resulting geometric error.
At runtime, hardware chooses between mesh, micro, splat and glint from projected size, the camera’s error target, hysteresis and minimum hold time.

This is not merely an architectural intention. The LOD-selection portion of GEOM.MESHFETCH already exists as zhao_geom_lod, is differential-tested against the reference implementation, and takes five clocks per instance evaluation.

So the correct instruction is:

Author exactly one excellent Zixxtrixx model. Do not author a second low-detail model and do not art-direct individual LOD rungs.

The compiler derives the lower representation and measures its error. Hardware selects the representation per camera at runtime.

There is one nuance worth preserving: hardware selects the rung; the asset compiler derives the micro geometry. Hardware does not invent a decimated mesh each frame. But from the modelling agent’s point of view, there is only one model.

The same principle should govern the texture:

one highest-quality source atlas;
packer-generated mip chain;
TMU-selected mip at runtime;
no separately hand-painted “far texture.”
2. What “reduce the polygons” means after this correction

The one authored model should still be retopologized intelligently:

remove the duplicate head/throat shell;
make the main shell form the skull;
use fewer circumference segments along ordinary trunk sections;
spend vertices around the eyes, neck hook, tight S reversal and fin roots;
replace tubular fins with proper planar blade topology;
add smooth normals so roundness comes from shading rather than thirty flat-shaded sides.

But there is no second model target.

A reasonable result might be one production model around 1,500–2,000 triangles, subject to the visual comparison. From that one model, the compiler derives the micro rung. If smooth normals are added, the compiler must also generate or recompute correct normals for the derived micro geometry. No artist touches it manually.

3. The current salto is a showcase clip, not a production attack system

The larger correction is the attack architecture.

Right now build_attack() is one fixed 220-key monolith. It bakes all of this into one clip:

one compression curve;
one coiling curve;
one fixed number of rotations;
one 12-metre lift curve;
one fixed forward curve;
one fixed 30-degree downward spear direction;
one fixed ground-impact key;
one five-second buried hold;
one fixed extraction and recovery.

That is an excellent Ground Dive showcase preset. It is not the general Zixxtrixx attack.

A real combat attack cannot assume:

the target is on the ground;
the target is below the apex;
exactly three flips are appropriate;
the spear must point down and forward;
contact happens at clip key 56;
the result must remain embedded in terrain.

A flying enemy might require:

a higher launch;
more rotations because the flight lasts longer;
a forward shot;
an upward shot;
a diagonal intercept;
no ground burial at all;
a mid-air impact recoil;
a miss-and-recover branch.

The attack therefore needs to become programmable choreography.

4. The lasting architecture
                   SIMULATION / GAMEPLAY TRUTH

target + target velocity + terrain + Zixxtrixx position
                              │
                              ▼
                        AttackPlanner
                              │
                              ▼
                          AttackPlan
          trajectory, timings, turns, spear axis, impact mode
                              │
                 ┌────────────┴────────────┐
                 ▼                         ▼
          ChoreographyPlayer        Collision / events
          shared pose phases        actual hit decision
                 │
                 ▼
       GEOM.POSE shared palette cache
                 │
                 ▼
        one per-instance root trajectory
        + one full 3D root orientation
                 │
                 ▼
            skin / render / hardware LOD

The essential separation is:

Shared local deformation

The bones describe what the animal is doing with its body:

compressing the S;
coiling;
holding the coil;
unrolling into a spear;
flexing on impact;
reforming the S.

These can remain shared compressed clip phases, decoded and cached by GEOM.POSE. The existing pose contract explicitly says clip selection and clip clocks are simulation truth, while the hardware cache serves shared (type, clip, frame) poses.

Per-instance root choreography

The attack plan describes where the whole creature goes and which way it points:

root position;
root velocity;
full 3D orientation;
total spin turns;
attack vector;
target intercept point.

This must be per instance and must live outside the shared skeletal pose.

That preserves the pose-cache economy. Two Zixxtrixxes can share the same “coiled body, frame 7” palette while one flies upward toward a bird and another dives down into the terrain.

5. Add a full 3D choreography-root transform

The current reference CreatureInstance has:

x/y/z;
a single yaw facing;
ground tilt;
bulk;
animation;
LOD.

It has no general 3D root orientation.

That is why the current salto puts the entire somersault rotation on bone 0 inside the clip. It works for one authored path but makes target-dependent flight awkward.

Add a generic per-instance choreography transform:

ChoreoRoot
    translation: vec3fx
    orientation: quat16 or equivalent full 3D rotation

Conceptually, the creature’s world transform becomes:

world translation
× ordinary facing
× choreography-root orientation
× ground tilt when grounded
× bulk scale
× shared skeletal pose

While airborne, ground tilt should be disabled or frozen at takeoff.

This is not a Zixxtrixx-only hack. The same root lane will support:

flying creatures;
creatures thrown by explosions;
knockback tumbles;
aerial attacks;
creatures falling from islands;
giant grab-and-throw sequences.

And it still obeys the repository’s central economy: the CPU is not uploading thirty-two individual bone matrices. It supplies one root transform for one creature, while GEOM.POSE continues sharing the local palette.

6. Split the salto into seam-matched pose phases

Do not create one pre-baked clip for every possible target height, direction and flip count.

Instead, create reusable local-body phases:

attack_compress
attack_release_to_coil
attack_coil_hold
attack_unroll_to_spear
attack_spear_flex
attack_ground_stick
attack_air_hit
attack_recover_to_s

The repository deliberately uses hard-cut clip transitions and a rich 64-slot vocabulary rather than an animation blending unit. Therefore these phase clips must have bit-identical seam poses:

end of compression equals start of release;
end of release equals coil hold;
coil hold loops exactly;
unroll begins at the exact coil pose;
spear branches begin at the exact straight pose;
recovery ends at the canonical S.

The asset compiler should enforce seam hashes rather than trusting the eye.

Move the actual flips out of the clip

The coiled body can remain in a shared coil pose.

The per-instance choreography root rotates that coil:

spin_angle(t) = planned_turns × eased_flight_phase

That means:

one flip;
three flips;
five flips;
faster or slower flips;
a different spin plane;

all use the same local coil pose.

The current attack spends clip frames rotating bone 0 through a fixed number of turns. That is precisely the part that needs to move into the programmable root layer.

During unrolling, smoothly blend the root orientation from its current spin orientation toward the planned spear orientation. At spear commitment, the body’s long axis and the planned attack vector must agree.

7. The AttackPlan

A deterministic fixed-point plan should contain approximately:

target position and velocity
predicted intercept position
launch duration
compression tier
compression duration
hold duration
release duration
flight duration
spin axis
total spin turns
unroll duration
spear direction
spear acceleration / duration
impact mode
recovery mode

It should be created by the simulation when the attack begins.

The planner does not need an expensive continuously homing solution. A clean game rule would be:

Predict or choose the target intercept.
Permit limited aim correction during the coiled flight.
Lock the spear vector when unrolling begins.
From spear commitment onward, Zixxtrixx is a projectile, not a guided missile.

That gives the move commitment, readability and counterplay.

8. The spring launch needs more time

The current pass technically added anticipation, but it is still brief: approximately six keys of compression and three keys of hold before release.

For the production phase, give the compression enough screen time to explain the height:

Settle

About 3–5 keys. Weight gathers; fins begin closing.

Compression

About 12–18 keys, or 0.4–0.6 seconds at the 30 Hz authored rate.

Do not merely deepen one lobe. Author a genuinely compressed-S centreline:

lower the upper arch;
pull the rear forward;
draw the head inward;
compress both lobes;
shorten the animal visibly;
bring the tail beneath the body;
keep the grounded contact planted.
Hold

About 4–6 keys.

The pose should become almost still. This is where the audience recognizes the loaded spring.

Release

About 6–10 keys.

Extension should travel from tail/rear through the middle into the head. The root should not simply begin moving upward because a lift curve says so; the body should visibly push it upward.

The stronger/higher the planned attack, the planner may choose a stronger compression tier or slightly longer hold. But use a small number of authored compression tiers rather than generating arbitrary unique bone poses per creature.

